      <memberdef kind="function" id="classServer_1a30362b937b125d27916cf595bfc4c1a7" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>int</type>
        <definition>int Server::run</definition>
        <argsstring>()</argsstring>
        <name>run</name>
        <qualifiedname>Server::run</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Prints the banner, configures and runs Drogon, then drains the worker pools. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Configures and runs the Drogon application, then drains the worker pools.</para>
<para>Blocks in Drogon&apos;s <ref refid="classServer_1a30362b937b125d27916cf595bfc4c1a7" kindref="member">run()</ref> until the event loops stop. On the way out it shuts down <ref refid="classDbExecutor" kindref="compound">DbExecutor</ref> first and <ref refid="classPasswordWorker" kindref="compound">PasswordWorker</ref> second, because database tasks may hand work to the password worker. Any std::exception from setup or teardown is reported through <ref refid="classMessage" kindref="compound">Message</ref> and turned into EXIT_FAILURE. <simplesect kind="return"><para>EXIT_SUCCESS after a clean run and shutdown, EXIT_FAILURE on a reported exception. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>SIGTERM handling is disabled (see the .disableSigtermHandling() call in the implementation), so shutdown is driven by the framework event loops stopping rather than by Drogon&apos;s own signal handler. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="classServer_1a419137d4cb2f6fdeee5e3982da4fd1d7" kindref="member">print_banner()</ref>, <ref refid="classStore" kindref="compound">Store</ref>, <ref refid="classDbExecutor" kindref="compound">DbExecutor</ref>, <ref refid="classPasswordWorker" kindref="compound">PasswordWorker</ref></para>
</simplesect>
Registers the listener, thread count, upload path, log level, a custom 404 page and two global advice hooks: the pre-routing hook short-circuits OPTIONS and rejects uncommon methods, and the pre-sending hook applies CORS headers to every response. When db_config_.async is set, an <ref refid="classAsyncDbClient" kindref="compound">AsyncDbClient</ref> is registered in the <ref refid="classStore" kindref="compound">Store</ref> before the event loops start. Control returns from drogon::app().<ref refid="classServer_1a30362b937b125d27916cf595bfc4c1a7" kindref="member">run()</ref> only after the event loops stop. <simplesect kind="return"><para>EXIT_SUCCESS on a clean run and shutdown, EXIT_FAILURE if setup or shutdown throws std::exception. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>The post-run shutdown order is deliberate: <ref refid="classDbExecutor" kindref="compound">DbExecutor</ref> is drained before <ref refid="classPasswordWorker" kindref="compound">PasswordWorker</ref> because database tasks may enqueue password work. Both happen here, while the <ref refid="classStore" kindref="compound">Store</ref> singletons still exist, rather than in static destructors. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="classServer_1a419137d4cb2f6fdeee5e3982da4fd1d7" kindref="member">print_banner()</ref>, <ref refid="namespaceCors_1a8bdb2553f14222ee4cacfb5628907590" kindref="member">Cors::apply()</ref>, <ref refid="classStore_1a0607fb3b583c06ca910ed6c9881e790c" kindref="member">Store::getObject()</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
